# Documentation Standards

## Documentation Strategy

### 1. Comprehensive Coverage
- Document all major features and systems
- Provide both quick reference and detailed guides
- Include examples and use cases
- Keep documentation current with code changes

### 2. Multiple Levels
- Quick start guides for immediate use
- Detailed documentation for deep understanding
- API documentation for developers
- User guides for end users

### 3. Accessibility
- Use clear, simple language
- Include code examples
- Provide step-by-step instructions
- Use consistent formatting

## README Documentation

### 1. Project Overview
- Clear project description
- Key features and capabilities
- Technology stack overview
- Getting started instructions

### 2. Quick Start
- Minimal setup instructions
- Basic usage examples
- Common commands
- Troubleshooting tips

### 3. Feature Documentation
- Detailed feature descriptions
- Configuration options
- Usage examples
- Best practices

### 4. Development Guide
- Development environment setup
- Contributing guidelines
- Testing procedures
- Deployment instructions

## Technical Documentation

### 1. Architecture Documentation
- System architecture overview
- Component interactions
- Data flow diagrams
- Design decisions and rationale

### 2. API Documentation
- Endpoint descriptions
- Request/response formats
- Authentication requirements
- Error handling

### 3. Configuration Documentation
- All configuration options
- Environment-specific settings
- Default values and recommendations
- Configuration validation

### 4. Database Documentation
- Schema documentation
- Table relationships
- Indexing strategies
- Migration procedures

## Code Documentation

### 1. Inline Comments
- Explain complex logic
- Document assumptions
- Provide context for decisions
- Keep comments current

### 2. Function Documentation
- Purpose and behavior
- Parameters and return values
- Usage examples
- Error conditions

### 3. Class Documentation
- Class purpose and responsibilities
- Public interface documentation
- Usage patterns
- Dependencies and relationships

## User Documentation

### 1. User Guides
- Step-by-step instructions
- Screenshots and examples
- Common tasks and workflows
- Troubleshooting guides

### 2. Feature Guides
- Detailed feature explanations
- Configuration options
- Best practices
- Advanced usage

### 3. FAQ and Support
- Common questions and answers
- Known issues and workarounds
- Support contact information
- Community resources

## Maintenance

### 1. Documentation Updates
- Update documentation with code changes
- Review documentation regularly
- Remove obsolete information
- Add missing documentation

### 2. Documentation Review
- Verify accuracy of documentation
- Check for broken links
- Ensure consistency
- Validate examples

### 3. Documentation Testing
- Test documentation examples
- Verify setup instructions
- Validate configuration examples
- Test troubleshooting procedures

## Formatting Standards

### 1. Markdown Guidelines
- Use consistent heading structure
- Include table of contents for long documents
- Use code blocks for examples
- Include links to related documentation

### 2. Code Examples
- Use syntax highlighting
- Include complete, runnable examples
- Provide context for examples
- Test all code examples

### 3. Visual Elements
- Use diagrams when helpful
- Include screenshots for UI documentation
- Use consistent image formatting
- Provide alt text for accessibility

## Documentation Organization

### 1. File Structure
- Organize by topic and audience
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Group related documentation
- Provide navigation between documents

### 2. Cross-References
- Link between related documents
- Provide context for references
- Keep links current
- Use descriptive link text

### 3. Version Control
- Track documentation changes
- Include documentation in code reviews
- Maintain documentation history
- Tag documentation versions

## Quality Assurance

### 1. Accuracy
- Verify all information is correct
- Test all examples and procedures
- Validate configuration options
- Check for technical accuracy

### 2. Completeness
- Ensure all features are documented
- Include all configuration options
- Provide complete examples
- Cover all use cases

### 3. Clarity
- Use clear, simple language
- Avoid jargon and acronyms
- Provide context for technical terms
- Use consistent terminology

## Accessibility

### 1. Language
- Use simple, clear language
- Avoid technical jargon
- Provide definitions for technical terms
- Use consistent terminology

### 2. Structure
- Use clear headings and subheadings
- Provide table of contents
- Use lists and tables appropriately
- Maintain logical flow

### 3. Examples
- Provide concrete examples
- Include step-by-step instructions
- Use real-world scenarios
- Provide context for examples

## Internationalization

### 1. Language Support
- Consider multiple languages
- Use clear, translatable language
- Avoid cultural references
- Provide language-specific examples

### 2. Cultural Considerations
- Use culturally neutral examples
- Consider different date/time formats
- Use appropriate units of measurement
- Consider different naming conventions

## Documentation Tools

### 1. Documentation Generators
- Use appropriate tools for documentation
- Automate documentation generation where possible
- Maintain tool configurations
- Keep tools up to date

### 2. Documentation Hosting
- Choose appropriate hosting platform
- Ensure accessibility and searchability
- Maintain documentation availability
- Provide backup and recovery

### 3. Documentation Metrics
- Track documentation usage
- Monitor documentation quality
- Gather user feedback
- Improve documentation based on metrics
